+   // Block size to prepare a hosted plugin at. This MUST match the block size
+   // the engine will actually feed processBlock, not the pool's 4096 ceiling:
+   // preparing at 4096 while the device delivers ~448-frame callbacks is a
+   // prepared-vs-actual mismatch that spec-loose plugins choke on - a streaming
+   // sampler (DecentSampler) comes out glitched and a block-size-sensitive host
+   // (Cardinal / VCV Rack) can hang. The mac AU path never hit this because the
+   // original prepared at 4096 there too and AUs tolerate it; the Windows JUCE
+   // VST3 backend does not, and the working fork prepared at the real device
+   // block for exactly this reason.
+   //
+   // AudioDeviceBufferFrames() returns the running WASAPI buffer size (the exact
+   // capacity the render loop caps each callback to - see AudioDeviceWin.cpp),
+   // so a callback can never exceed it: preparing here is always >= what
+   // PluginRender is later handed, so its "numFrames > maxFrames -> silence"
+   // guard can never trip from this. 512 is the fallback when no device is open
+   // yet (headless prepare); the clamp keeps it in the pool's legal range.
+   int PluginPrepareBlockFrames()
+   {
+#if defined(__APPLE__)
+      // mac AU path is unchanged on purpose: it has always prepared at the pool
+      // ceiling and AUs tolerate it, so the working mac build stays byte-for-byte
+      // as it was. Only the Windows JUCE VST3 backend needs the real block.
+      return kAudioMaxBlockFrames;
+#else
+      int block = (int)Platform::AudioDeviceBufferFrames();
+      if (block <= 0)
+         block = 512;
+      return std::clamp(block, 64, kAudioMaxBlockFrames);
+#endif
+   }
 }
 
